relative humidity: provides an indication of the tendency of timber to maintain a high moisture content for prolonged periods and therefore to fall within a specific wood corrosivity category.

In particular, the weather window indicates the level of moisture to which timber and connections may be exposed during construction. This affects both atmospheric corrosivity, which is generally better known and already considered in material selection, and, above all, wood corrosivity, namely the moisture conditions in the material that may accelerate the corrosion of fasteners embedded within.
